 <div class="ramadan-guide-facts"><div><span>Beginning of the lunar month</span><strong>At sunset</strong><p>First fast follows at dawn</p></div><div><span>Length of a lunar month</span><strong>29 or 30 days</strong><p>Confirm with local announcement</p></div><div><span>Daily fasting interval</span><strong>Fajr to Maghrib</strong><p>Based on your local time calculation</p></div></div>

 <section class="ramadan-guide-section" id="ramadan-dates" aria-labelledby="ramadan-dates-heading">
  <h3 id="ramadan-dates-heading">When does Ramadan begin and end?</h3>
  <p>Ramadan is the ninth month of the Islamic lunar calendar. <a href="https://quran.com/2/185" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quran 2:185</a> identifies Ramadan as the month of the Quran and sets out the obligation of fasting, with allowances and provisions discussed in the verse. <a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:1909" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 1909</a> connects starting and ending the month’s fast with the crescent and gives guidance when visibility is obstructed.</p>
  <p>In a Hijri calendar, the religious date changes at <strong>sunset</strong>. If a community announces that Ramadan begins on the evening before a Monday, Monday is the first civil <strong>fasting day</strong>. This tool asks for the <em>first Gregorian fasting day</em>, not the evening of the crescent announcement.</p>
  <p>The calendar’s initial suggestion uses an algorithmic <strong>Umm al-Qura</strong> date. It is explicitly labelled as an estimate, since communities may follow local sightings, other regional sighting policies or a different computed calendar. Neither the calculator nor the algorithm can independently confirm a sighting.</p>
  <div class="ramadan-guide-caution"><strong>Before printing:</strong> Check your local authority’s first-day announcement. Near month-end, confirm whether the final fast is day 29 or day 30 and when Shawwal/Eid al-Fitr begins. A pre-generated month is not a final religious calendar.</div>
 </section>

 <section class="ramadan-guide-section" id="ramadan-how-to" aria-labelledby="ramadan-how-heading">
  <h3 id="ramadan-how-heading">How to create a reliable 29- or 30-day timetable</h3>
  <ol class="ramadan-guide-steps">{steps.map(s=><li><h4>{s.title}</h4><p>{s.detail}</p></li>)}</ol>
  <p>The table displays the Ramadan day number, Gregorian weekday/date, calculated Fajr, calculated Maghrib, and approximate fast duration. An optional early preparation reminder can be shown in a separate column; it is not the fast’s start time.</p>
 </section>

 <section class="ramadan-guide-section" id="ramadan-dawn" aria-labelledby="ramadan-dawn-heading">
  <h3 id="ramadan-dawn-heading">Suhoor, imsak, Fajr and Iftar: which times matter?</h3>
  <p><a href="https://quran.com/2/187" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quran 2:187</a> gives the daily fasting boundaries: eating and drinking until dawn becomes clear, then completing the fast until night. In the timetable, <strong>Fajr</strong> estimates the true-dawn start and <strong>Maghrib</strong> estimates sunset, when iftar begins.</p>
  <p><strong>Suhoor</strong> is the pre-dawn meal, encouraged in <a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:1923" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 1923</a>. Some calendars show an earlier <strong>imsak</strong> as a precaution or planning signal. There is no universal additional ten-minute religious cutoff established by the timetable itself. When in doubt about your mosque’s dawn assessment, use qualified local guidance.</p>
  <div class="ramadan-worked-example"><h4>A hypothetical daily schedule</h4><div class="ramadan-example-metrics"><div><span>Optional reminder</span><strong>05:00</strong></div><div><span>Calculated Fajr</span><strong>05:10</strong></div><div><span>Calculated Maghrib</span><strong>19:12</strong></div></div><p>A ten-minute reminder at 05:00 helps someone finish their meal, but <strong>does not change the calculated Fajr at 05:10</strong>. An iftar estimate of 19:12 is tied to local sunset, not the Isha prayer. These sample clock times do not describe any real location or date.</p></div>
  <p><a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:1957" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 1957</a> encourages timely breaking of the fast once sunset has been established. The separate <a href="/tools/fasting-times/">Suhoor &amp; Iftar Times</a> guide discusses daily reminders, narrated duas and common fasting questions in more depth.</p>
 </section>

 <section class="ramadan-guide-section" id="ramadan-methods" aria-labelledby="ramadan-methods-heading">
  <h3 id="ramadan-methods-heading">Why do Ramadan schedules differ between mosques and cities?</h3>
  <div class="ramadan-guide-grid"><article><h4>Location and horizon</h4><p>Dawn and sunset depend on latitude, longitude, the date, elevation and local horizon. The tool calculates astronomical estimates, not observations from a particular minaret or hill.</p></article><article><h4>Fajr angles and methods</h4><p>Organisations adopt different twilight angles or local conventions. The calculator offers several established software presets and a first-day comparison, but it cannot decide which one a specific mosque uses.</p></article><article><h4>High latitudes and polar conditions</h4><p>During northern summer, astronomical twilight can behave unusually or fail to reach a selected angle. The available approximation rules are selectable and disclosed, never treated as a binding religious verdict.</p></article><article><h4>Daylight saving time</h4><p>The displayed clocks use the named place’s IANA timezone on <em>each day</em>. For example, Europe/London normally switches between GMT and BST; do not manually add another hour to a displayed BST time.</p></article></div>
  <p>The underlying implementation uses the open-source <a href="https://github.com/batoulapps/adhan-js" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Adhan JavaScript astronomical library</a>. Its outputs are UTC instants; the tool converts them to local clock times for the selected timezone. If an instant is implausible for that civil day, the corresponding row is marked <strong>Unavailable</strong>, not silently substituted with sunrise or another city’s time.</p>
  <p class="ramadan-guide-caution">Especially in northern England, Scotland and polar regions, a software setting may not match the practice followed by local religious authorities. Resolve discrepancies before relying on the timetable for the actual fast.</p>
 </section>

 <section class="ramadan-guide-section" id="ramadan-example" aria-labelledby="ramadan-example-heading">
  <h3 id="ramadan-example-heading">Worked example: planning a provisional 29- or 30-day Ramadan</h3>
  <p>Suppose someone enters <strong>8 February 2027</strong> solely as an <em>illustrative first fasting day</em>. The date is not presented as an official crescent or Ramadan announcement.</p>
  <div class="ramadan-example-metrics"><div><span>First day entered</span><strong>8 Feb 2027</strong></div><div><span>If 29 days</span><strong>8 Mar 2027</strong><small>Last scheduled day</small></div><div><span>If 30 days</span><strong>9 Mar 2027</strong><small>Last scheduled day</small></div></div>
  <p>The calculator generates exactly 29 or 30 consecutively dated rows. If their mosque subsequently confirms a different starting day or final length, they change those inputs and regenerate their paper, CSV or calendar copy. The daily times also change if the location or calculation method changes. The example does <strong>not</strong> supply sample Fajr/iftar clocks, since actual times require a specific place and method.</p>
  <p>A whole-month plan may cross an official clock change in some years. Because each Fajr and Maghrib is calculated as a separate UTC instant and formatted using the named timezone, the printed local times and imported calendar events can represent such transitions without applying one constant offset throughout the month.</p>
 </section>

 <section class="ramadan-guide-section" id="ramadan-exports" aria-labelledby="ramadan-exports-heading">
  <h3 id="ramadan-exports-heading">Printing, CSV and calendar events — what each export contains</h3>
  <ul><li><strong>Print / Save PDF:</strong> uses the browser’s print dialog to produce a clean, full-month timetable with location, timezone, method and provisional-date warnings. Choose PDF in the print dialog to save a file.</li><li><strong>CSV:</strong> includes all days, the calculated times, approximate duration and any optional reminder, along with location, timezone, method, high-latitude and polar settings. Unavailable cells are explicitly marked; user-supplied labels are protected from spreadsheet formula execution.</li><li><strong>ICS calendar:</strong> includes Fajr and Maghrib events as true UTC instants, with calculation context. It is disabled if any day lacks a valid pair of times. A calendar import will not automatically refresh after dates or settings change.</li></ul>
  <p>Your city and prayer-method preferences may be stored in your browser using the site’s existing settings. The timetable is computed on the device; there is no Ramadan account, cloud sync or collection of a personal fasting history. Choosing an export creates a local file. For a browser with shared access, review its saved location settings.</p>
  <p>For the relationship between calendar dates and evening moon sightings, see the <a href="/tools/hijri-converter/">Hijri Converter</a> and the <a href="/guides/ramadan-dates-and-timetables/">Ramadan dates reference</a>. For daily fasting duas and reminders, see <a href="/tools/fasting-times/">Suhoor &amp; Iftar Times</a>.</p>
 </section>

